a level appropriate forhuman consumption. Because the pro
cess uses osmotic potentialOPratherthanhydraulicpressure,
the process is referred to as forward osmosis (“FO), which
can be thought of as running reverse osmosis (“RO”) in
reverse. Becausetheprocess uses osmotic potential instead of
hydraulic pressure, it has been shown to have fewer mem
brane fouling problems that are inherent to RO and micro
filtration.
